---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TdeeCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/TdeeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="tdee-calculator">
  <TdeeCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to work out your TDEE</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter sex, height, weight and age so the calculator can estimate your basal rate.</li>
      <li>
        Choose an activity level. Read the descriptions, not only the labels: most errors come from this
        step.
      </li>
      <li>
        Optionally add your body fat percentage to change the BMR formula from Mifflin-St Jeor to
        Katch-McArdle.
      </li>
      <li>
        Read your maintenance number, then pick a target from the goal table to lose, keep or gain weight.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What TDEE is made of</h2>
    <p>
      Total daily energy expenditure is all the energy your body uses in 24 hours. It has four parts:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Component</th>
            <th scope="col">Share of TDEE</th>
            <th scope="col">What it is</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Basal metabolic rate (BMR)</td>
            <td>60–70%</td>
            <td>Keeping you alive at complete rest</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Thermic effect of food (TEF)</td>
            <td>about 10%</td>
            <td>Digesting and storing what you eat; protein costs the most, about 20–30% of its own calories</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Non-exercise activity (NEAT)</td>
            <td>15–30%</td>
            <td>Walking, fidgeting, standing, housework, commuting; the most variable component</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Exercise activity (EAT)</td>
            <td>0–15%</td>
            <td>Deliberate training; usually smaller than people expect</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      NEAT is the most variable part. Between two people of the same size it can differ by 2,000 kcal a day,
      and it falls without notice when you diet: you sit more, take the lift and gesture less. This is a
      large part of why weight loss slows faster than the arithmetic predicts.
    </p>

    <h2>TDEE activity multipliers</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Level</th>
            <th scope="col">Multiplier</th>
            <th scope="col">What it looks like</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Sedentary</td>
            <td>1.2</td>
            <td>Desk job, drive everywhere, under 5,000 steps, no training</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Lightly active</td>
            <td>1.375</td>
            <td>Desk job plus 1–3 workouts a week, or 7,000–10,000 steps a day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Moderately active</td>
            <td>1.55</td>
            <td>3–5 real training sessions a week, or a job on your feet all day</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Very active</td>
            <td>1.725</td>
            <td>Hard training 6–7 days a week, or manual work plus regular sessions</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Extra active</td>
            <td>1.9</td>
            <td>Two sessions a day, or an athlete in a heavy block, or heavy labour plus training</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If you are between two levels, pick the lower one. An overestimate of activity is the most common
      reason a calculated deficit gives no weight loss.
    </p>

    <h2>Two mistakes that break the number</h2>
    <p>
      Counting exercise twice: the multiplier already includes your training. If you add the 600 kcal your
      watch reported for a run on top of a 1.55 multiplier, you count the same run twice. Fitness trackers
      also over-report exercise burn by 20 to 90 percent, depending on the device and the activity, so the
      error is larger still.
    </p>
    <p>
      Calories you did not log: cooking oil, the milk in three coffees, a few bites while you cook dinner. In
      controlled studies, free-living adults under-report their intake by 20 percent on average, without
      intent. If a deficit does not work, this is usually the reason, more often than metabolism.
    </p>

    <h2>How to find your real TDEE</h2>
    <p>
      An equation gives a first estimate. The empirical method takes three weeks and is much more accurate:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Weigh yourself every morning, after the bathroom, before eating. Log all seven days.</li>
      <li>Log everything you eat, weighed where practical, without changing your habits.</li>
      <li>
        After 14 to 21 days, compare the first and last seven-day weight averages. If weight is flat,
        your average intake is your TDEE.
      </li>
      <li>
        If it changed, adjust: each kilogram of change per week equals about 1,100 kcal a day of surplus or
        deficit (about 7,700 kcal per kilogram ÷ 7 days), or about 500 kcal a day for each pound per week.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      After that, recalculate when your weight changes by about 5 percent, because maintenance changes with
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>Choosing a target from your TDEE</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Fat loss: 10 to 25 percent below maintenance. Twenty percent suits most people. A deficit larger
        than 25 percent for long periods costs muscle, training quality and adherence.
      </li>
      <li>
        Maintenance: useful between diet phases. Two to four weeks at maintenance after a long cut restores
        appetite hormones and training performance.
      </li>
      <li>
        Muscle gain: 5 to 15 percent above maintenance. Beginners can build muscle near maintenance.
        Experienced lifters need the surplus but gain more slowly, so a larger surplus mostly adds fat.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      With a target, use the macro calculator to turn it into grams of protein, carbohydrate and fat, and the
      calorie calculator to estimate how long a goal weight will take.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
